Jove wrote:

>   Worst case Restore Ability can be blanked to water.  Those who like to
> polymorph can also polymorph it.  These are also true for a spellbook of
> restore ability.

It is my considered opinion that there should be no items in the game
that are absolutely useless, with the exception of a few bad types to
increase the cost of identifying through experimentation, and even many
of them have clever uses: there are times when it's better to be blind
than have sight, times when it's better to be confused than
clear-headed, and even a specific case when you're better off
hallucinating.

To clarify, I say that there should be circumstances with *all* kinds
of potions and scrolls when it is better to have that item than a
potion of water/blank scroll.  It is possible to see actions, in the
mysterious addings of features by the DevTeam, that imply similar
attitudes.  The existence of green slimes give a use to scrolls of
fire, for example (although they can also be used as a weak attack
scroll).

The gameplay balance for blanking items comes from the usage of turns
to perform the blanking, the usage of opportunities (fountains,
cancellation charges) that cannot be made up, the restriction of its
use to only certain types of items (unicorn horns), and/or the effort
or equipment required to blank the item or lack of control over the
process (stripping and jumping into a pool, or dipping over a pool with
water walking boots).  As Nethack has continued to evolve, and YANIs
are added to the game, more and more methods of creating water or blank
scrolls have been added, making these various kinds of losses
substantially less of an issue -- if one of the methods is
inappropriate for your game, you could always use another.  That leaves
the loss of the item as the remaining substantial cost to item
blanking, yet there are too many items where this is a no-brainer.

